I don't think 'burn-out' is a very accurate translation of 'Squib':
Rowling's use of it probably comes from the English expression "damp
squib", meaning someone who won't join in the fun. A squib was a kind
of indoor firework, so if it got damp it could never be used - not
something that has "burned out" its usefulness. Note that the Harry
Potter definition of a Squib as a child born into a wizard family who
has no potential to become a wizard himself; so using it for an
outcast wizard isn't very accurate from that direction either, if it
matters.

Squib conduct: play a wizard, but never cast any spells?
